---
// Why Foundations Move in New Mexico. Reimagined reference page per the
// user's creative-license grant (same as Symptom Guide) -- built from the
// user-pasted code blocks: hero, the full .tls-body reference document
// (TOC, symptom matrix, 26-item Cause Library w/ dropdown + accordions,
// when-not-to-underpin cards, industry-gaps cards, intervention spectrum,
// prevention checklist, honest caveat), contact-close, and a page-specific
// footer service-area paragraph.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Why Foundations Move in New Mexico — TLS Foundations';
const summary =
  "Every foundation problem traces back to one of three things: the soil's behavior changed, the moisture regime changed, or the load on the foundation changed -- often more than one at once. Which driver is at work determines whether underpinning helps or makes things worse, and that can't be read off the surface symptoms alone, since heave and settlement often look identical. The Cause Library below catalogs 26 specific causes across six mechanisms -- from lost soil support to deep geologic hazards -- because the right response depends entirely on which one is actually happening.";
const description =
  "Foundations move because soil moves, and in New Mexico no two soils move the same way. The reference on causes, symptoms, and underpinning.";

const serviceAreaText = `Foundations move because soil moves — and in New Mexico, no two soils move the same way. The causes are specific: collapsible soil settlement, expansive clay heave, expansive shale movement, hydrocompaction, caliche/cemented soil bearing failure, soil piping, karst subsidence, evaporite dissolution, frost heave, post-tension slab failure, poorly compacted fill, cut-and-fill transition movement, plumbing leaks beneath slabs, irrigation-driven moisture change, tree root extraction, hillside creep, seismic activity along the Rio Grande Rift, and regional groundwater subsidence. The symptoms look similar — foundation cracks, sticking doors, uneven floors, slab gaps, wall separation, diagonal cracking at corners. The causes are not similar. Getting the diagnosis wrong produces a worse outcome than the original condition. Many New Mexico foundations don't need underpinning. The ones that do, we repair.`;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does every foundation crack mean I need underpinning?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No. Cosmetic curing cracks, seasonal thermal movement, and stabilized historic settlement typically need no structural repair at all. Underpinning is one response among several -- drainage correction, leak repair, and monitoring are often the honest first step.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: "What's the difference between heave and settlement?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Settlement is soil pulling down; heave is soil pushing up. They can look identical from cracking alone, but the correct response is opposite in each case -- underpinning fixes settlement, not heave.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why does the same crack pattern have so many possible causes in New Mexico?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'New Mexico spans sixteen geologically distinct basins, each with its own soil behavior -- expansive clay, collapsible soil, karst, evaporite dissolution, and more. Visual inspection alone cannot distinguish between them; that requires investigation.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="full"
  ogImage="/images/why-foundations-move-hero.webp"
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Why Foundations Move"
  nextPage={{ path: '/expansive-soil', name: 'Expansive Soils' }}
  footerProps={{ serviceAreaLabel: "That's a lot of ways a home can move", serviceAreaText }}
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <img class="hero-photo" src="/images/why-foundations-move-hero.webp" alt="New Mexico high desert soil formation" fetchpriority="high" />
    <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
    <div class="tls-hero-wrap">
      <div class="tls-hero-box">
        <span class="tls-hero-headline">Foundations move.</span>
        <span class="tls-hero-sub">(Doesn't always mean yours needs repair.)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="tls-body">
    <div class="container">
      <!-- SECTION 1 — OPENING -->
      <section id="introduction">
        <span class="eyebrow">TLS Foundations Reference</span>
        <h1>Why Foundations Move in New Mexico</h1>
        <div class="subhead">And why many don't need underpinning. The ones that do, we repair.</div>
        <p>Foundations don't "fail" because they crack. They move and crack because the soil supporting them changes. In New Mexico, that change is almost always driven by moisture — too much in one place, not enough in another, or introduced where the ground never expected it.</p>
        <p>Underpinning is one possible response to foundation movement. It is not always the right one. Many homes in New Mexico show signs of movement that can be managed without structural intervention — through drainage correction, leak repair, or simply monitoring over time.</p>
        <p>This page explains the full range of reasons foundations move, when underpinning is the appropriate response, and when it isn't. It is written for homeowners who want honest answers and for the engineers who serve them.</p>
        <p>It is not a diagnostic tool. It does not replace professional evaluation. It gives you the vocabulary to ask better questions — and to recognize when the answers you're getting don't add up.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Table of Contents (static) -->
      <div class="toc" id="table-of-contents">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#first-principle">First Principle</a></li>
          <li><a href="#symptom-matrix">Symptom Matrix</a></li>
          <li><a href="#cause-library">Cause Library</a></li>
          <li><a href="#when-not">When Underpinning Isn't the Answer</a></li>
          <li><a href="#industry-gaps">What the Industry Gets Wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#spectrum">Intervention Spectrum</a></li>
          <li><a href="#prevention">Prevention & Maintenance</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 2 — FIRST PRINCIPLE -->
      <section id="first-principle">
        <h2>Foundation Movement Is Not the Problem — It's the Result</h2>
        <p>Every foundation problem traces back to one of three things: the soil changed behavior, the moisture regime changed, or the load on the foundation changed. Sometimes two at once. Sometimes all three.</p>
        <p>The soil beneath your home is not static. It responds to water, weight, temperature, and time. When those conditions shift — a pipe leaks, drainage patterns change, a new addition adds weight — the soil responds. The foundation follows.</p>
        <p>Understanding which of these three drivers is at work is the first step toward knowing what to do about it. That understanding comes from investigation, not from looking at cracks. <a href="/symptom-guide">See our Symptom Guide for a detailed breakdown of what you're seeing.</a></p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 — SYMPTOM MATRIX -->
      <section id="symptom-matrix">
        <h2>What You're Seeing vs. What May Be Happening</h2>
        <p>Foundation symptoms look similar. The causes behind them are not. Here's what the most common signs may point to — and why the first step is always investigation.</p>

        <div class="symptom-matrix-wrap">
          <div class="symptom-header-row">
            <div class="symptom-col-head">What You See</div>
            <div class="symptom-col-head">What May Be Happening</div>
            <div class="symptom-col-head">Is Underpinning the Answer?</div>
            <div class="symptom-col-head">The Honest First Step</div>
          </div>

          <div class="symptom-row">
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What You See</span><strong>Diagonal cracks, doors sticking seasonally</strong></div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What May Be Happening</span>Soil volume change — expansive clay.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">Is Underpinning the Answer?</span>Rarely first step. Moisture management often resolves.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">The Honest First Step</span>Evaluate drainage, irrigation. <a href="#expansive-clay" class="jump-to-cause" data-accordion-link="expansive-clay">Learn about expansive clay →</a> | <a href="/expansive-soil">Full article →</a></div>
          </div>

          <div class="symptom-row">
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What You See</span><strong>Sudden settlement after heavy rain or snowmelt</strong></div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What May Be Happening</span>Collapsible soil — structure collapses when wet.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">Is Underpinning the Answer?</span>Often yes, but water source must be stopped first.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">The Honest First Step</span>Find and fix water source. <a href="#collapsible-soils" class="jump-to-cause" data-accordion-link="collapsible-soils">Collapsible soils →</a> | <a href="/collapsible-soil">Article →</a></div>
          </div>

          <div class="symptom-row">
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What You See</span><strong>Localized cracking near kitchen/bath</strong></div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What May Be Happening</span>Plumbing or sewer leak beneath slab.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">Is Underpinning the Answer?</span>No while leak active. Repair first.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">The Honest First Step</span>Pressure test water lines; camera sewer. <a href="#plumbing-leaks" class="jump-to-cause" data-accordion-link="plumbing-leaks">Plumbing leaks →</a></div>
          </div>

          <div class="symptom-row">
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What You See</span><strong>Cracks on sloped lots or near arroyos</strong></div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What May Be Happening</span>Lateral soil movement or erosion.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">Is Underpinning the Answer?</span>Not alone; slope must be stabilized.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">The Honest First Step</span>Evaluate slope, drainage, arroyo proximity.</div>
          </div>

          <div class="symptom-row">
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What You See</span><strong>Hairline cracks unchanged for years</strong></div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">What May Be Happening</span>Concrete curing shrinkage or thermal movement.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">Is Underpinning the Answer?</span>No.</div>
            <div class="symptom-cell"><span class="symptom-cell-label">The Honest First Step</span>Monitor; seal if desired. <a href="/common-concerns">Common Concerns →</a></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 4 — CAUSE LIBRARY -->
      <section id="cause-library">
        <h2>The Cause Library</h2>
        <p>Foundation movement in New Mexico has many causes. They are organized here by mechanism — what is actually happening to the soil — because understanding the mechanism is the prerequisite for understanding the fix.</p>
        <p><em>Every cause described here requires professional evaluation to diagnose correctly. No visual inspection alone can distinguish between them.</em></p>

        <select class="cause-dropdown" id="cause-dropdown">
          <option value="">— Jump to a cause —</option>
          <optgroup label="Loss of Support">
            <option value="collapsible-soils">Collapsible Soils</option>
            <option value="a2-content">Poorly Compacted Fill</option>
            <option value="a3-content">Cut-and-Fill Transition Movement</option>
            <option value="a4-content">Landfill and Undocumented Fill Sites</option>
            <option value="a5-content">Subsurface Erosion and Soil Piping</option>
            <option value="a6-content">Consolidation of Deep Alluvium</option>
            <option value="a7-content">Bearing Failure from Load Changes</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Soil Volume Change">
            <option value="expansive-clay">Expansive Clay Soils (including Mancos Shale, Sulfate Heave, Caliche)</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Water Drivers">
            <option value="plumbing-leaks">Plumbing and Sewer Leaks Beneath Slab</option>
            <option value="c2-content">Poor Surface Drainage & Concentrated Runoff</option>
            <option value="c3-content">Irrigation and Landscaping</option>
            <option value="c4-content">Tree Root Moisture Extraction</option>
            <option value="c5-content">Altered Moisture from Adjacent Development</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Deep Geologic Hazards">
            <option value="d1-content">Evaporite Dissolution & Karst Subsidence</option>
            <option value="d2-content">Hillside Creep & Slope Instability</option>
            <option value="d3-content">Seismic Activity & Rio Grande Rift</option>
            <option value="d4-content">Regional Groundwater Changes</option>
            <option value="d5-content">Arroyo and Flash Flood Erosion</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Construction Deficiencies">
            <option value="e1-content">Inadequate Foundation Design</option>
            <option value="e2-content">Inadequate Reinforcement & Structural Capacity</option>
            <option value="e3-content">Post-Tension Cable Failure</option>
            <option value="e4-content">Prior Repair Failure</option>
            <option value="e5-content">Adobe & Non-Standard Foundations</option>
          </optgroup>
          <optgroup label="Site & Environmental">
            <option value="f1-content">Frost Heave</option>
            <option value="f2-content">Vibration-Induced Settlement</option>
            <option value="f3-content">Burrowing Animal Activity</option>
          </optgroup>
        </select>

        <div class="jump-links">
          <a href="#group-a" data-group="group-a">Loss of Support</a>
          <a href="#group-b" data-group="group-b">Soil Volume Change</a>
          <a href="#group-c" data-group="group-c">Water Drivers</a>
          <a href="#group-d" data-group="group-d">Deep Geologic Hazards</a>
          <a href="#group-e" data-group="group-e">Construction Deficiencies</a>
          <a href="#group-f" data-group="group-f">Site & Environmental</a>
        </div>

        <!-- GROUP A -->
        <div id="group-a" class="group-header"><h3>Group A — Loss of Support</h3><p><em>The soil can no longer carry the weight. Underpinning is often the right answer.</em></p></div>

        <div class="accordion-item" id="collapsible-soils">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="collapsible-soils-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapsible-soils-content">A1. Collapsible Soils</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="collapsible-soils-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Some New Mexico soils have a loose, open structure — almost like a honeycomb — held together by weak bonds that dissolve when water reaches them. These soils can support a house for decades while they stay dry. When water is introduced from any source — a leak, irrigation, a storm — the bonds break and the soil drops. Settlement can be sudden and dramatic.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Extremely common statewide, particularly on valley margins, alluvial fan deposits, and mesa edges where development has moved from irrigated valley floors onto previously dry higher ground.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Yes — once collapse has occurred, the soil structure is permanently altered. Piers driven through the collapsed zone to stable material below are the standard response. But the water source must be found and controlled first, or collapse will continue in untreated areas.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> This is routinely misdiagnosed as 'normal settling' or blamed on poor compaction. The mechanism is fundamentally different — it is not gradual compression under weight, it is a sudden collapse of the soil structure, triggered by water. National contractor training barely covers it because it's an arid-climate phenomenon. This is arguably the most underappreciated foundation hazard in New Mexico.</p>
            <p><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/collapsible-soil">Collapsible Soils article →</a> | <a href="/glossary#hydrocompaction">Glossary: Hydrocompaction →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="a2-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a2-content">A2. Poorly Compacted Fill</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="a2-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> When a building site is graded, excavated material is used as fill to create a level pad. If this fill isn't properly compacted in controlled lifts, it compresses under the weight of the structure over time. Settlement is typically uneven because fill thickness varies across the site. Some fill contains debris, construction waste, or organic material that decomposes and creates voids.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Everywhere homes are built, but especially on hillside lots, subdivisions cut into mesas, sites where arroyos or drainages were filled, and rapid-growth developments.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Yes. The fill will not improve on its own. Piers through the fill to competent native soil or bedrock are the standard solution.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Without as-built records, it's difficult to know fill is present without investigation. The homeowner typically has no idea their lot was filled. Contractors often label this 'natural settlement' without checking.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="a3-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a3-content">A3. Cut-and-Fill Transition Movement</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="a3-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Houses built into hillsides often sit partly on undisturbed cut soil and partly on compressible fill. These two portions bear on fundamentally different materials. The fill side settles; the cut side doesn't. Differential movement is virtually guaranteed.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Foothills developments, Santa Fe hillsides, East Mountains, any area where lots were created by grading into slopes.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Often yes, especially when fill is deep. The fill side will typically require deeper piers than the cut side.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="a4-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a4-content">A4. Landfill and Undocumented Fill Sites</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="a4-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Homes built on or near former landfill sites face ongoing settlement as buried waste decomposes, compresses, and creates voids. Gas migration from decomposition can also affect soil chemistry and moisture patterns. Older landfills may not appear on any map the homeowner has access to.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Scattered across the state. Municipal records may or may not document former disposal sites, particularly informal ones from earlier decades.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Depends on depth and extent. Deep piers to stable material below the fill may work for localized settlement. Widespread active decomposition may make the site unsuitable for standard residential repair.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Homeowners often have no idea their property sits on or near a former landfill. Investigation is the only way to identify it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="a5-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a5-content">A5. Subsurface Erosion and Soil Piping</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="a5-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Water moving through erodible soils creates underground channels that carry soil particles away, forming voids beneath foundations. In New Mexico's climate of long dry periods interrupted by intense monsoon rainfall, this process can accelerate dramatically during storm events.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Common in dispersive soils (sodium-rich clays), fine-grained alluvial deposits, and loess. Rio Grande Valley and statewide.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Depends. Void filling may be the first priority. The water pathway must be interrupted. Underpinning alone does not solve subsurface erosion.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Invisible until it manifests as sudden settlement or sinkhole-like depressions. Rarely considered in standard repair evaluations.</p>
            <p><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/glossary#soil-piping">Glossary: Soil piping →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="a6-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a6-content">A6. Consolidation of Deep Alluvium</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="a6-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Naturally deposited soft clays and silts compress under the weight of a structure over time. This is a load-driven process — the soil is squeezing water from its pore spaces under sustained pressure. Settlement can continue for years after construction but typically slows over time.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Rio Grande floodplain, major tributary floodplains, and ancient lake bed deposits in closed basins.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Yes, if the settlement is differential and exceeds structural tolerance. If uniform and within tolerance, monitoring is more appropriate.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Confused with other settlement types. The distinguishing feature: it's predictable, load-related, and decelerates over time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="a7-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="a7-content">A7. Bearing Failure from Load Changes</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="a7-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Additions, second stories, remodels that remove load-bearing walls, or changes that concentrate weight in new locations can exceed what the original soil and foundation were designed to support.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Often yes — the foundation needs deeper or broader support to handle the new loads.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Blamed on 'bad soil' when the real issue is a load change the original design didn't anticipate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- GROUP B -->
        <div id="group-b" class="group-header"><h3>Group B — Soil Volume Change (Active Zone)</h3><p><em>Soils expand, shrink, or react chemically with moisture. Underpinning is often NOT the first response.</em></p><p>Beneath every foundation, moisture fluctuates seasonally — this is the <a href="/glossary#active-zone">active zone</a>. Most movement originates here, driven by moisture.</p></div>

        <div class="accordion-item" id="expansive-clay">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="expansive-clay-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="expansive-clay-content">B1. Expansive Clay Soils (including Mancos Shale, Sulfate Heave, Caliche)</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="expansive-clay-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Clay minerals that swell when wet, shrink when dry. Cyclical movement. Damage comes from repeated cycles over years. In New Mexico, these clays often come from specific geological formations that make them particularly reactive.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Statewide; severe in Rio Grande Valley, San Juan Basin, areas underlain by weathered Cretaceous shales. Caliche is common on alluvial fans and piedmont slopes.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Sometimes — when differential movement has caused structural distress that moisture control cannot reverse. But many expansive clay cases are better addressed first through drainage correction, irrigation management, and stabilizing the moisture envelope around the foundation. Underpinning without addressing moisture treats the symptom, not the cause.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Heave is frequently misread as settlement. Homeowners see cracks and think their house is sinking when part of it is actually being pushed up. Underpinning a heaving foundation does not fix the problem — it fights the earth. The honest first step is always to evaluate and manage the moisture before reaching for piers.</p>
            <p><strong>Regional variations:</strong> In the San Juan Basin, Mancos Shale weathers into highly expansive clay that can be more severe than typical expansive soils; this weathered zone can extend many feet below grade, and the boundary between weathered and stable material is not uniform. In areas with Permian evaporites or gypsiferous soils, sulfate heave can occur — a chemical reaction between calcium stabilizers and sulfate-bearing soils that creates progressive, irreversible heave that does not reverse with drying. Caliche — a hard calcium carbonate layer — can provide false bearing if fractured or sitting on unstable material. These conditions require specialized geotechnical investigation.</p>
            <p><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/expansive-soil">Expansive Soils article →</a> | <a href="/glossary">Glossary: Heave, Caliche →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- GROUP C -->
        <div id="group-c" class="group-header"><h3>Group C — Water and Moisture Drivers</h3><p><em>Water is the trigger for nearly every cause. These cases are primarily water problems, not soil problems.</em></p></div>

        <div class="accordion-item" id="plumbing-leaks">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="c1-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="c1-content">C1. Plumbing and Sewer Leaks Beneath Slab</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="c1-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Leaks from supply, sewer, or drains saturate soil, causing heave, collapse, or erosion. Sewer leaks are especially insidious — they are deep, persistent, and often go undetected for years.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> No while leak active. Repair leak first, allow soil to stabilize, then reassess.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Many repair contracts signed without sewer camera inspection. A plumber fixes the pipe; a contractor sells piers — but nobody evaluates whether the soil will stabilize on its own once the water stops.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="c2-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="c2-content">C2. Poor Surface Drainage & Concentrated Runoff</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="c2-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Water ponds against foundation due to improper grading, missing gutters, short downspouts, or hardscape directing runoff toward the building.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Almost never. Drainage correction should always come first; in many cases, it eliminates the movement entirely.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Less profitable to fix gutters than sell piers. This is the single most controllable factor in foundation performance.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="c3-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="c3-content">C3. Irrigation and Landscaping</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="c3-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Sprinklers or drip lines against foundation create moisture differential between irrigated perimeter and dry native soil.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Almost never. Move irrigation away; establish a dry buffer zone.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Homeowners rarely connect watering habits to foundation cracks.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="c4-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="c4-content">C4. Tree Root Moisture Extraction</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="c4-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Large trees extract moisture from soil, causing shrinkage and settlement.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Rio Grande Valley, irrigated areas with cottonwoods, elms.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Rarely as first step. Root barriers, pruning, tree management first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="c5-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="c5-content">C5. Altered Moisture from Adjacent Development</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="c5-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> New construction, paving, or landscaping on neighboring properties changes water infiltration patterns.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Rarely. Identify what changed and correct if possible.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Focus stays on damaged house; nobody looks at neighbor's property.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- GROUP D -->
        <div id="group-d" class="group-header"><h3>Group D — Deep Geologic Hazards</h3><p><em>Instability deeper or broader than surface corrections can address. Some unique to NM.</em></p></div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="d1-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="d1-content">D1. Evaporite Dissolution & Karst Subsidence</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="d1-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Gypsum, salt dissolve at depth, creating voids and sinkholes.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Southeastern NM (Permian Basin), Santa Rosa, Tularosa Basin.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Rarely. Standard residential piers cannot bridge deep voids. This is a site suitability question requiring geologic investigation.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Gradual subsidence attributed to 'normal settling.' Contractors rarely discuss cases where no practical localized fix exists.</p>
            <p><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/glossary#subsidence">Glossary: Subsidence →</a> | <a href="/glossary#karst">Glossary: Karst →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="d2-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="d2-content">D2. Hillside Creep & Slope Instability</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="d2-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Slow downhill soil movement, applying lateral force to foundations.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Sandia/Manzano foothills, Santa Fe hillsides, Jemez Mountains, mesa edges.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Only after slope stabilization. Piers alone will not resist lateral movement.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Symptoms mimic differential settlement; clue is consistent downhill pattern.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="d3-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="d3-content">D3. Seismic Activity & Rio Grande Rift</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="d3-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Earthquakes can cause soil densification or liquefaction.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Rift corridor — Albuquerque, Socorro, Santa Fe, Taos.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Post-earthquake settlement: yes. Fault rupture: underpinning doesn't address.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="d4-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="d4-content">D4. Regional Groundwater Changes</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="d4-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Decades of pumping draw down water tables, causing broad subsidence.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Albuquerque Basin, Mimbres, Estancia, lower Rio Grande.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Not typically; regional issue. Underpinning may stabilize individual structure but won't stop subsidence.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Almost universally missed at individual-home level. Systematically absent from contractor education.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="d5-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="d5-content">D5. Arroyo and Flash Flood Erosion</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="d5-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Lateral erosion from arroyos undermines foundations during flash floods.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Not typically. Requires channel stabilization, erosion protection.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- GROUP E -->
        <div id="group-e" class="group-header"><h3>Group E — Construction & Design Deficiencies</h3><p><em>Not all movement is soil — some is a design or materials problem.</em></p></div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="e1-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e1-content">E1. Inadequate Foundation Design</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="e1-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Foundation not matched to soil conditions — shallow footings, no geotech investigation.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Statewide; many older homes designed without soil testing.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Often yes — providing deep support that should have been there.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="e2-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e2-content">E2. Inadequate Reinforcement & Structural Capacity</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="e2-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Concrete without enough steel cracks instead of bridging gaps. Piers on unreinforced foundation may lift one section while adjacent stays put.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Partially; structural reinforcement (carbon fiber, rebar) often needed too.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Contractors install piers assuming foundation transfers loads; if unreinforced, repair fails.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="e3-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e3-content">E3. Post-Tension Cable Failure</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="e3-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Loss of tension in post-tension slab leads to cracking, settlement, or bowing.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Rarely. Forensic investigation, structural repair (carbon fiber, partial replacement).</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Many PT slab cracks sold as 'settlement requiring piers' without checking cable tension.</p>
            <p><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/glossary#post-tension-slab">Glossary: Post-tension slab →</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="e4-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e4-content">E4. Prior Repair Failure</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="e4-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Previous underpinning failed — piers not deep enough, wrong bearing, etc.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Yes, as second repair. Evaluate and install deeper piers.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Presented as 'new' problem when it's actually failure of prior repair.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="e5-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="e5-content">E5. Adobe & Non-Standard Foundations</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="e5-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Adobe walls on adobe/rubble foundations; moisture causes material degradation that mimics settlement.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Northern NM, Albuquerque historic neighborhoods.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> With caution. Requires specialized engineering; often material repair and moisture protection better.</p>
            <p><strong>What's commonly missed:</strong> Degradation misread as settlement. The wall isn't sinking — the bottom of the wall is dissolving.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- GROUP F -->
        <div id="group-f" class="group-header"><h3>Group F — Site & Environmental Triggers</h3></div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="f1-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="f1-content">F1. Frost Heave</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="f1-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Freeze-thaw cycles lift shallow footings.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Mountain communities — Taos, Angel Fire, Ruidoso.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Rarely. Insulation, drainage, proper footing depth.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="f2-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="f2-content">F2. Vibration-Induced Settlement</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="f2-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Traffic, construction, rail lines densify loose soils.</p>
            <p><strong>Where in NM:</strong> Corridors near I-25, I-40, rail lines.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Sometimes; soil densification may be better.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="accordion-item">
          <button class="accordion-header" data-acc="f3-content" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="f3-content">F3. Burrowing Animal Activity</button>
          <div class="accordion-content" id="f3-content" aria-hidden="true">
            <p><strong>What it is:</strong> Prairie dogs, gophers create tunnels and voids.</p>
            <p><strong>Underpinning appropriate?</strong> Not typically. Void filling and animal management first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 — WHEN NOT -->
      <section id="when-not">
        <h2>When Underpinning Is Not the Answer</h2>
        <p><em>This is the section no other contractor publishes.</em></p>
        <div class="no-underpin-grid">
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Cosmetic cracking from concrete curing</strong> No repair needed.</div>
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Thermal movement</strong> Cracks that open/close seasonally are normal.</div>
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Uniform settlement that has stabilized</strong> Monitor.</div>
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Active heave without net settlement</strong> Moisture control, not piers.</div>
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Moisture problems not yet corrected</strong> Fix water first.</div>
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Slab curling</strong> Slab replacement or grinding.</div>
          <div class="no-underpin-card"><strong>Addition differential movement</strong> Isolation joints.</div>
        </div>
        <p>The honest alternative for many: improve drainage, repair leaks, manage irrigation, stabilize slopes, reinforce concrete, or monitor. Many New Mexico homes can be managed without structural intervention.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 — INDUSTRY GAPS -->
      <section id="industry-gaps">
        <h2>What the Industry Gets Wrong</h2>
        <div class="industry-grid">
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Moisture is the primary driver</strong> — not soil type alone. Moisture control doesn't sell piers, so it's underemphasized.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Underpinning without moisture correction usually fails.</strong> Biggest unspoken truth.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Collapsible soils are invisible in national training.</strong> In NM, collapsible soil is as common as expansive clay.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Heave and settlement look identical but require opposite responses.</strong> Underpinning fixes settlement, not heave.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Contractors sell solutions before diagnosing problems.</strong> The cause is assumed, not investigated.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Piers can fail — and nobody talks about it.</strong> Skin friction, caliche cap failure, corrosion.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>The dry season creates a sales opportunity.</strong> Many gaps close during monsoon — honest contractors say so.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>'Lifting' is not always the right goal.</strong> Sometimes arresting movement is more honest and less destructive.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Geotechnical investigation is treated as optional.</strong> Visual-only repairs fail when the cause is deeper.</div>
          <div class="industry-card"><strong>Post-repair monitoring is rarely discussed.</strong> No benchmark, no follow-up — no way to know if it worked.</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 7 — SPECTRUM -->
      <section id="spectrum">
        <h2>The Intervention Spectrum</h2>
        <div class="spectrum">
          <div class="spectrum-step"><strong>Monitor</strong> Minor, historical, within tolerance.</div>
          <div class="spectrum-step"><strong>Moisture & Site Correction</strong> Drainage, leaks, irrigation, grading.</div>
          <div class="spectrum-step"><strong>Soil Improvement</strong> Grouting, void fill, chemical stabilization.</div>
          <div class="spectrum-step"><strong>Structural Reinforcement</strong> Carbon fiber, rebar, slab strengthening.</div>
          <div class="spectrum-step"><strong>Underpinning</strong> Deep piers to stable material.</div>
        </div>
        <p>A company that only offers the last option will recommend it for every problem. <a href="/foundation-repair-methods">See detailed explanations of each repair method.</a></p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 8 -->
      <section>
        <h2>Why Evaluation Comes First</h2>
        <p>No reliable repair recommendation can be made from symptoms alone. Cracks, tilting, sticking doors can be caused by a dozen different mechanisms.</p>
        <p>A proper evaluation includes site history, elevation surveys, soil borings, moisture source identification (including sewer camera), and structural analysis. It's the only honest path to a solution that actually solves the problem.</p>
        <p>TLS Foundations requires this step. Our affiliated geotechnical firm, Sandia GEO, operates independently from repair sales — evaluation driven by evidence, not by what we need to sell.</p>
        <div class="soft-cta">
          <p>Have questions? We're happy to talk — no pressure, just answers. <a href="/contact-us">Contact TLS Foundations →</a></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 9 — PREVENTION -->
      <section id="prevention">
        <h2>Prevention and Maintenance</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Maintain positive grading directing water away from foundation.</li>
          <li>Extend downspouts at least 6 feet from foundation.</li>
          <li>Keep irrigation and plantings away; establish 3-foot dry buffer zone.</li>
          <li>Repair leaks promptly; test plumbing (including sewer lines).</li>
          <li>Avoid over-watering on expansive or collapsible soils.</li>
          <li>Monitor cracks, doors, floors seasonally; photograph and date.</li>
          <li>If you remove a large tree, watch for soil rebound (swelling).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>None of this costs much. All of it matters.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 10 — CAVEAT -->
      <section>
        <h2>The Honest Caveat</h2>
        <p>This page explains conditions that can lead to foundation movement. It is not a diagnostic tool. Several different mechanisms can create identical-looking cracks. Only site-specific investigation confirms what's happening beneath your home.</p>
        <p>Determining the correct response requires understanding whether movement is ongoing, what's driving it, and whether that driver can be corrected without structural intervention.</p>
        <p>This page gives you vocabulary to ask better questions. It does not give you the ability to diagnose your own home.</p>
        <p class="video-link">Prefer it on camera? <a href="/soil-movement" class="body-link">Soil &amp; Movement</a> covers this same ground in three short films.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="contact-close">
